# SYNOPSIS

```text
match=pci vendor=id device=id program=path post=srv [msi=n]
match=pci class=class program=path post=srv [msi=n]
match=acpi hid=id program=path [post=srv] [clock]
```

# DESCRIPTION

A driver manifest says which devices a driver drives, and what devmgr(8) gives it.
The manifests are the files `boot/drv/*.ndb` in the repository, one for each driver, installed in the boot image as `/boot/drv/*.ndb`.
They are written by hand, and only devmgr reads them: once, at its start, after it has scanned PCI, through its namespace, where svcd mounts the boot image on `/`.
It reads each file in `/boot/drv` in bootfs(4)'s order, by name, and each file's records in order.

A manifest is ndb records (see ndb(6)), one record for each match.
A record matches PCI functions or the ACPI devices bus-acpi(8) reports, as its `match=` says, and every function or device it matches gets its own instance of the driver.
One device gets one driver, through its post: the first match to take the post's claim has it.

A manifest with an ndb error anywhere, or a key not defined below, is used not at all: devmgr says so, naming the file and the key.

Numbers are decimal, without leading zeros, or hexadecimal after `0x`, in lower case.

@node=keys title="Keys"

: `match`
  The bus: `pci` or `acpi`.
  A record without it is ignored.
: `vendor`
  A PCI match's vendor ID, with `device`, as `0x1af4`.
: `device`
  A PCI match's device ID, with `vendor`, as `0x1042`.
: `class`
  A PCI match's class, subclass and programming interface, as one number: `0x010802` is any NVMe controller, whoever made it.
  It is used only when the record has no `vendor` and `device`.
: `hid`
  An ACPI match's hardware ID, at most 15 bytes, as bus-acpi reports it from `_HID`: `PNP0B00` is the PC's CMOS clock.
  It must be equal, byte for byte.
: `program`
  The driver's path in the boot image, from `/`, at most 63 bytes, such as `/boot/bin/drv-nvme`.
  Required.
: `post`
  The post the driver serves, `/srv/<srv>` without `/srv/`, at most 25 bytes.
  It must be one svcd gives devmgr a claim on, by a `claim=` in devmgr's service manifest (see svc(6)).
  A post ending in `#` is numbered: every PCI match, of every record whose post has that prefix, gets the prefix and a number, `disk0`, `disk1`, ..., in the order devmgr found the functions, so `/srv/disk0` is the first disk found, virtio or NVMe.
  A PCI match needs a post; an ACPI match may have none.
: `msi`
  How many MSIs a PCI match's driver gets, at most 8; none without it.
: `clock`
  A flag on an ACPI match: the driver keeps time, and is given a channel to tell devmgr the time on, from which devmgr sets the kernel's wall clock.

@node=given title="What the driver is given"

The driver's spawn message (see {svc(6)#spawn}) holds its device as handles and records, its post's server end as `listen` and the console as `console`; devmgr(8) says what each handle is.
Its records are:

: `bar`
  For each memory BAR given, `bar=<n> size=<bytes>`: the handle `bar<n>` is BAR <n>, whole, rounded up to pages.
: `msi`
  For each MSI, `msi=<n> address=<address> data=<data>`: the handle `msi<n>`, and the message the driver programs the function with.
: `address`
  An MSI's address.
: `data`
  An MSI's data.
: `io`
  For each of an ACPI device's `_CRS` port ranges, `io=<n> base=<port> size=<ports>`, as `io=0 base=0x70 size=8`: the handle `io<n>`, an IoRange.
: `mem`
  For each `_CRS` memory range, `mem=<n> base=<address> size=<bytes>`: the handle `mem<n>`, a physical VMO of the pages that hold it.
: `irq`
  For each `_CRS` interrupt, `irq=<n> base=<line> size=<n>`: the handle `irq<n>`.
: `base`
  A resource's first port, address or interrupt, as `_CRS` gave it.
: `size`
  A BAR's or resource's bytes, or ports.
: `fadt`
  For a clock driver, `fadt century=<reg>`: the FADT's CMOS century register, when it has one.
: `century`
  The century register's index.
: `start`
  Which start of the driver this is: 1, then 2 after a restart, and so on.
: `cmdline`
  The kernel command line, whose `<program>.<key>=<value>` words are the driver's options, as `drv-nvme.reset=50`.

# EXAMPLES

The four manifests in `boot/drv`:

```ndb
match=pci class=0x010802 program=/boot/bin/drv-nvme post=disk# msi=8
match=acpi hid=PNP0B00 program=/boot/bin/drv-rtc-cmos clock
match=pci vendor=0x1af4 device=0x1042 program=/boot/bin/drv-virtio-blk
    post=disk# msi=1
match=pci vendor=0x1af4 device=0x1041 program=/boot/bin/drv-virtio-net
    post=ether0 msi=2
```

The claims in devmgr's own manifest, `boot/svc/devmgr.ndb`, which the posts above must be among:

```ndb
service=devmgr program=/boot/bin/devmgr resource acpi console cmdline restart
mount=/ srv=bootfs
claim=ether0
claim=disk0
claim=disk1
claim=disk2
claim=disk3
claim=acpi
```

# FILES

: `/boot/drv/*.ndb`
  The manifests, in the boot image.
: `boot/drv/*.ndb`
  The manifests in the repository.

